# Chapter 13

# Layout Manager

## Overview

The Layout Manager is the central controller of the LMS frontend.

Every page in the system uses one shared layout.

Instead of placing JavaScript inside each page, the Layout Manager initializes all global functionality before the current page is loaded.

File Location

```text
wwwroot/js/core/layout.js
```

The Layout Manager is responsible only for application-wide functionality.

Feature-specific logic is never placed inside Layout.

---

# Layout Responsibilities

The Layout Manager currently performs the following tasks:

* Load shared HTML components
* Initialize authentication
* Initialize authorization
* Display current user information
* Configure the header
* Configure the sidebar
* Configure the footer
* Configure notifications
* Configure search
* Configure shopping cart
* Configure theme
* Configure logout
* Bind global events

This keeps every page lightweight.

---

# Layout Initialization Flow

```text
Layout.initialize()

↓

Load Shared Components

↓

Initialize Authentication

↓

Initialize Authorization

↓

Load Current User

↓

Update Layout

↓

Bind Global Events

↓

Return Control

↓

PageRegistry.initialize()
```

Every page follows this same initialization sequence.

---

# Global Events

Only application-wide events belong in Layout.

Examples include:

```text
Logout

Global Search

Theme Switching

Window Resize

Sidebar Toggle

Notifications
```

Feature events such as Login or Register belong inside their respective modules.

---

# Chapter 14

# Shared Components

The LMS uses reusable HTML components.

These components are loaded dynamically.

Current shared components include:

```text
loader.html

header.html

topbar.html

sidebar.html

footer.html

modals.html
```

Instead of duplicating HTML across multiple pages, the application loads these files dynamically.

Advantages include:

* Reduced duplication
* Easier maintenance
* Faster updates
* Consistent appearance
* Reusable layout

---

# Shared Layout Structure

```text
_Layout.cshtml

│

├── Loader

├── Topbar

├── Header

├── Sidebar

├── RenderBody()

├── Footer

└── Modals
```

Every MVC page shares this layout.

---

# Dynamic User Interface

The layout automatically updates according to the user's authentication state.

Anonymous users see:

* Login
* Register

Authenticated users see:

* Logout
* Current User Name
* Authorized Navigation

This behavior is controlled through authentication attributes.

---

# Authentication Attributes

The LMS frontend uses HTML attributes to control visibility.

Supported attributes include:

```html
data-auth

data-anonymous

data-role

data-permission
```

Examples

Authenticated content

```html
<div data-auth>
```

Anonymous content

```html
<div data-anonymous>
```

Role-based navigation

```html
<li data-role="Administrator">
```

Permission-based buttons

```html
<button data-permission="Course.Create">
```

The Layout Manager automatically evaluates these attributes after authentication is initialized.

