# Chapter 16

# Authentication

Authentication was implemented using JWT.

The frontend communicates with the backend through the Authentication API.

Current endpoints include:

```text
POST /Auth/login

POST /Auth/register

POST /Auth/change-password

POST /Auth/forgot-password

POST /Auth/reset-password

GET /Auth/current-user/{id}
```

Future endpoints include:

```text
POST /Auth/logout

POST /Auth/refresh-token
```

---

# Login Flow

The complete login process is shown below.

```text
User

↓

Login Form

↓

Validation

↓

Account Events

↓

Account Service

↓

Account API

↓

LMS.API

↓

JWT Generated

↓

API Response

↓

Storage Service

↓

Security Service

↓

Layout Refresh

↓

Dashboard
```

This architecture separates every responsibility.

---

# Login Response Standard

The login API returns a standardized response.

```json
{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Login successful",
  "data": {
    "accessToken": "...",
    "refreshToken": "...",
    "expires": "...",
    "user": {
      "userId": "...",
      "fullName": "...",
      "email": "...",
      "roles": [],
      "permissions": []
    }
  },
  "errors": []
}
```

This response format became the project standard.

---

# Storage After Login

When authentication succeeds:

StorageService saves:

```text
Access Token

Refresh Token

Current User
```

SecurityService then recognizes the user as authenticated.

---

# Logout

The current logout implementation performs the following steps:

```text
Logout Button

↓

Account Events

↓

Account Service

↓

Security Service

↓

Storage Service

↓

Redirect Login
```

The browser immediately removes:

* Access Token
* Refresh Token
* Current User

The layout refreshes automatically.

Future versions will invalidate the refresh token on the server.

