# Chapter 17

# Authorization

The LMS uses two authorization mechanisms.

Role-Based Authorization

Permission-Based Authorization

---

# Roles

Current roles include:

```text
Administrator

Instructor

Student

Parent
```

Roles determine broad access to the application.

---

# Permissions

Permissions provide fine-grained control.

Examples include:

```text
Dashboard.View

Courses.View

Course.Create

Course.Edit

Lessons.View

Assignments.View

Certificates.Download
```

Permissions allow features to be enabled without creating additional roles.

---

# Security Service Methods

Current authorization methods include:

```text
hasRole()

hasAnyRole()

hasPermission()

hasAnyPermission()
```

These methods are used throughout the application to determine access.

---

# Automatic UI Authorization

The Layout Manager automatically evaluates:

```html
data-role

data-permission
```

Unauthorized controls are hidden without requiring page-specific JavaScript.

This keeps authorization centralized and consistent.

---

# Chapter 18

# Account Module

The Account module was the first complete feature implemented using the new architecture.

It serves as the reference implementation for all future modules.

Folder Structure

```text
features/account

api.js

service.js

events.js

validation.js

ui.js

login.js

register.js

forgot-password.js

reset-password.js

change-password.js
```

Every future feature module must follow this exact structure.

---

# Module Responsibilities

**api.js**

* API communication only

**service.js**

* Business logic
* Response processing
* Storage updates

**events.js**

* Event handling
* Form submission
* Button clicks

**validation.js**

* Client-side validation

**ui.js**

* Loading indicators
* Success messages
* Error messages
* Form management

**page.js**

* Initialize controls
* Bind events
* Register page

